/* ---- recap ---- */
{s:RC,k:'recap',term:'Recap',say:`Three panels. Plain descent: one update per epoch, and a learning rate too large diverges. ${P('mini-batch')} descent: random samples, an unbiased estimate, variance falling with the batch. And the numbers: ten iterations per epoch, two hundred updates.`,cues:{r1:'Plain descent',r2:'random samples',r3:'the numbers'},panels:[["Gradient Descent And","BLU",'down',['θ ← θ − η∇L; one epoch, one update.','Too-large η diverges — not a local minimum.'],'r1'],["Stochastic And Mini-Batch","ORG",'recycle',['B random samples per update; E[gB] = g.','Variance σ²/B; shuffle every epoch.'],'r2'],["The Numbers","GRN",'clock',['N = 1000, B = 100: 10 iterations per epoch,','200 updates in 20 epochs.'],'r3']],live:[0,1,2]},
/* ---- exam practice ---- */
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Why is the mini-batch gradient called unbiased?",say:`Question one. Why is the ${P('mini-batch')} gradient called unbiased? Pause the video and answer out loud.`,cues:{k:'Question one',q:'Why is the'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Why is the mini-batch gradient called unbiased?",say:'If every batch is drawn uniformly at random, the average over all batches comes back to the full gradient. That is what unbiased means.',cues:{a1:'every batch is drawn',a2:'comes back to the full gradient'},al:[["Each batch is drawn uniformly at random.",'a1'],["So E[gB] = g, the full gradient.",'a2']]},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"For L(θ) = θ², start at θ₀ = 2 with η = 0.2. Compute θ₁ and θ₂.",say:'Question two. For the loss theta squared, start at two with a learning rate of zero point two. Find the first two parameters. Pause and work it out.',cues:{k:'Question two',q:'For the loss'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"For L(θ) = θ², start at θ₀ = 2 with η = 0.2. Compute θ₁ and θ₂.",say:`The gradient is two ${P('theta')}. At two it is four, so the first parameter is two minus zero point eight: one point two. The gradient there is two point four, so the second parameter is zero point seven two.`,cues:{fk:'The gradient is two',k1:'At two it is four',k2:'the first parameter is',k3:'The gradient there is',k4:'the second parameter is',a:'zero point seven two'},calc:{f:['∇L = 2θ','θ ← θ − 0.2 ∇L'],hint:'Gradient first, then the step.',fk:'fk',steps:[["At θ₀ = 2","∇L = 2 × 2 = 4",'k1'],["θ₁","2 − 0.2 × 4 = 1.2",'k2'],["At θ₁ = 1.2","∇L = 2 × 1.2 = 2.4",'k3'],["θ₂","1.2 − 0.2 × 2.4 = 0.72",'k4']],live:[0,1,2,3]}},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"For L(θ) = θ² with update θ ← (1 − 2η)θ, find the range of η for which the iteration converges to θ = 0.",say:'Question three. The update multiplies the parameter by one minus twice the learning rate. For which learning rates does it settle at zero? Pause and find the range.',cues:{k:'Question three',q:'The update multiplies'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"For L(θ) = θ² with update θ ← (1 − 2η)θ, find the range of η for which the iteration converges to θ = 0.",say:'The parameter shrinks only if the magnitude of one minus two times the learning rate stays below one, which gives learning rates between zero and one. At exactly one the parameter just flips sign forever: two, minus two, two. Above one it diverges.',cues:{fk:'the magnitude of one minus',k1:'which gives learning rates between',k2:'At exactly one',k3:'Above one it diverges'},calc:{f:['θ ← (1 − 2η)θ','|1 − 2η| < 1'],hint:'Geometric: the ratio must be under one in size.',fk:'fk',steps:[["Convergence","|1 − 2η| < 1  →  0 < η < 1",'k1'],["At η = 1","θ ← −θ: 2, −2, 2, …",'k2'],["Above η = 1","|1 − 2η| > 1: diverges",'k3']],live:[0,1,2]}},
{s:LS,k:'figs',term:'Your notes',title:'For your notes',say:'Your free notes for this lesson are the PDF linked in the description: chapters two point one to two point two, both figures, key terms and a glossary. Redraw each figure from memory, then check it against the notes.',cues:{t:'Your free notes',f:'Redraw each figure'},tags:[["Free notes for this lesson (PDF)", "link in the description"], ["Chapters 2.1 to 2.2", "Gradient descent and its limitations; Stochastic and mini-batch gradient descent"], ["Key terms and glossary", "at the end of the notes"]],figs:[["Figure 2.1.1", "One pass over all the data gives exactly one gradient and one weight update; the dashed back edge is the next epoch."], ["Figure 2.2.1", "Three batches of four samples make one epoch; the order is reshuffled before the next epoch starts. The marked cells are the first sample in each batch."]],note:"Sources the book draws on: Goodfellow, Deep Learning (chapters 7 and 8); Bishop, Pattern Recognition and Machine Learning."},
{s:OUT,k:'outro',term:'Outro',say:`So remember: plain descent takes one update per epoch, ${P('mini-batch')} takes many cheap noisy ones, and a step size that is too large diverges. Next time, momentum and ${P('Nesterov')} accelerated descent. See you there!`,cues:{f:'So remember',w1:'plain descent takes one update',w2:'takes many cheap noisy ones',w3:'too large diverges',n:'Next time',s:'See you there'},words:[['ONE UPDATE PER EPOCH','BLU','w1'],['MANY CHEAP NOISY STEPS','ORG','w2'],['TOO BIG A STEP DIVERGES','GRN','w3']],next:["NEXT TIME", "Lesson 2", "Momentum and Nesterov accelerated gradient descent · Adaptive optimizers", "book"],end:'See you there!'}
